/**
 * Held-out MCP configs. Labels were assigned from public docs and the
 * published exposure model before the detection allowlist was read.
 * Do not edit a label to chase a score. This file is not a tuning set.
 *
 * Severity, per config, is the set a scanner should emit:
 *   critical: sensitive data can leave the machine, credentials can leave
 *     through execution and the network, source changes can reach production,
 *     or a shell can read files and touch untrusted content. A server whose
 *     documented tools already include that whole chain (arbitrary shell,
 *     pod exec against a cluster) is critical on its own.
 *   high: broad file access (home, root, Documents, Drive, or a server that
 *     edits files everywhere), untrusted content beside shell or code
 *     execution, or outbound messaging beside untrusted content. Production
 *     mutation alone (payments, cluster, writable hosted database) is high
 *     when the chain above is not already present.
 *   medium: one sensitive grant with no cross-boundary chain (one repo,
 *     a local database, a vendor API that only holds that vendor's data).
 *   none: no entry. Benign tools, scary names on benign packages, and
 *     disabled entries do not count. Loopback URLs are not egress.
 * The display name is not evidence. The package, command, URL, and declared
 * tools are. Declared tools count even on an unknown command.
 */
